        "content": "## Summary\n\n**Target:** `F:ml430-nat-add-modeq-left-e3b1fba9` — `Nat.add_modEq_left`\n\n**Formal statement:** `∀ {n a : ℕ}, n + a ≡ a [MOD n]`\n\n**Structural shape:** This is a modular equivalence statement asserting that `n + a` is congruent to `a` modulo `n`. It's a two-variable universal quantification over naturals with no dependencies.\n\n**Available premises:** The nat prelude contains:\n- One modEq theorem: `Nat.modEq_pow` (power congruence)\n- Three modEq-add theorems: `Nat.mod_eq_add`, `Nat.mod_eq_add_left`, `Nat.mod_eq_add_right` (congruence preservation under addition)\n- Basic addition properties: commutativity, associativity, cancellation\n- `Nat.add_pow_modeq_prime` (Fermat's little theorem variant)\n\n**Similar eligible facts:** The frontier contains closely related facts:\n- `F:ml430-nat-add-modeq-right-e2f11f21` (symmetric version, right addition)\n- `F:ml430-nat-modeq-add-left-e83f0700` (congruence combinator)\n- `F:ml430-nat-modeq-add-right-8e2ca0cc` (congruence combinator)\n- `F:ml430-int-add-modeq-left-ee732b5b` (integer version)\n- `F:ml430-int-add-modeq-right-e58108ee` (integer version, right)\n\n**Operations:** No registered operations target this fact yet.",
